## Container build

The root `Dockerfile` builds only `{{proj_axum}}`. Prepare the workspace first:

1. Check `[workspace.dependencies]` in `Cargo.toml`. `edgezero new` may have
   generated paths into the developer's EdgeZero checkout or unpinned Git
   references. Replace every EdgeZero entry with the same approved release tag
   or commit at `https://github.com/stackpop/edgezero.git`, preserving its
   feature settings. Keep paths between this application's crates.
2. Run `cargo generate-lockfile`, review the dependency sources with
   `cargo metadata --locked --format-version 1`, and commit this application's
   manifests and `Cargo.lock`. Do not copy EdgeZero's lockfile. These preparation
   commands may fetch dependencies; scaffolding itself does not.
3. Keep the Docker builder's Rust pin aligned with `.tool-versions`. If adding
   `rust-toolchain.toml`, use a matching exact quoted `[toolchain].channel`.
   Update both declarations and the reviewed builder image when changing Rust.
4. Review `.dockerignore` and add your app-specific config, secrets and state
   filenames. Keep required workspace manifests and build-time assets.

```sh
docker build -t app:local .
docker run --rm --read-only --cap-drop=ALL \
  --security-opt=no-new-privileges:true \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8787:8787 app:local
```

The image runs as UID/GID 10001 and binds `0.0.0.0:8787` inside the container.
Local development still defaults to localhost. The default app has no stores
and needs no writable mount. Apps using current embedded stores must provision
an explicit `/app/.edgezero` mount with permissions for UID 10001. No config or
keys are generated by the image. Embedded redb requires one writer.

This recipe proves packaging, not production readiness or bounded SIGTERM
shutdown. Application owners publish their own images. See the
[Axum guide](https://stackpop.github.io/edgezero/guide/adapters/axum)
for runtime limitations, optional asset builds and container checks.

